\section{Escaping the Bottleneck}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Motivation.}
\looseness=-1 No training-free self-extraction method achieves independence from the model's distributional prior (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). We test whether a structurally independent verifier, sharing neither architecture nor training data with the generator, provides corrective signal that escapes the same-model ceiling.

\paragraph{Method.}
\looseness=-1 We use DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, to classify (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\looseness=-1 Symbolic confirmation bias predicts that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). We test whether a structurally independent verifier can escape this bottleneck, with the expectation that combo gain should decrease as generator SC approaches or exceeds verifier accuracy.

\paragraph{Zero-shot results.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO and 55.00\% on ProofWriter. Combo $w{=}3$ yields $+2.94$\,pp on FOLIO (n.s.) and $+6.67$\,pp on ProofWriter for Mistral-7B ($p < 0.001$, significant after BH correction, $m{=}12$); all generators with SC $\geq 72\%$ show no significant improvement ($\Delta \leq +0.83$\,pp, $p > 0.4$). Same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution. Preliminary evidence suggests that zero-shot NLI escape requires the generator's SC to fall below the verifier's standalone accuracy.

\paragraph{Capability gap.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 47-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): only Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains significantly ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$); all three generators above the verifier (SC $\geq 72\%$) show no significant improvement ($\Delta \leq +0.83$\,pp). The pattern is consistent with a capability-gap model: combo gain decreases monotonically with generator SC, with breakeven near the verifier's standalone accuracy. The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures and six verifier-generator pairs (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.003$, $n{=}6$; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}; Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}). Per-label analysis confirms complementary error patterns that diminish as the generator improves (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-generator NLI escape with DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone, ProofWriter $n{=}600$). Combo gain decreases monotonically with generator capability. Only Mistral-7B (SC below the verifier) shows a significant gain.}
\label{tab:cross_generator_nli}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Generator & Family & SC & Gap & Combo $\Delta$ ($w{=}3$) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & Mistral & 39.33\% & $+$15.67 & $+$6.67\,pp & $<$0.001 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & LLaMA & 72.50\% & $-$17.50 & $+$0.83\,pp & 0.665 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & Qwen & 74.00\% & $-$19.00 & $+$0.67\,pp & 0.503 \\
Qwen3-14B & Qwen & 85.83\% & $-$30.83 & $+$0.50\,pp & 0.45 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Domain-adapted verifier.}
\looseness=-1 Fine-tuning DeBERTa on ProofWriter depth-5 OWA (99.67\% standalone) amplifies the escape: combo gains reach $+$12.67\,pp (Mistral-7B, $w{=}3$) and $+$11.67\,pp (LLaMA-8B), a $14\times$ amplification over LLaMA's zero-shot result ($+0.83 \to +11.67$\,pp), with near-total error complementarity ($364{:}2$ and $164{:}1$; Table~\ref{tab:finetuned-nli}, Appendix). Domain-adapted verification extends escape to generators with SC up to approximately 72\%, with monotonically decreasing gain, consistent with the prediction that the capability gap can be shifted by increasing verifier accuracy. A cost-aware cascade recovers 83--90\% of full-combo gain at ${\sim}35\%$ NLI cost (Appendix~\ref{app:drc}).

\section{The Symbolic Mirage}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. A shuffle control reveals this bias is \emph{structural}: randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ ($p > 0.7$), indicating that BR reflects the trace-count distribution rather than extraction-specific error. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on 94.4\% of problems (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting. This vacuity is $\alpha$-insensitive: agreement $\geq$99\% across $\alpha \in \{0.1, \ldots, 0.9\}$ (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}).

\looseness=-1 This pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.85\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $n_\text{err}$: number of incorrect instances on which BR is computed. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; BR estimates for high-accuracy models ($n_\text{err} < 40$) have wider confidence intervals; Gemini ($n_\text{err} \approx 12$) should be interpreted with particular caution. Process-level similarity ($\kappa_{\text{proc}}$) ranges from $0.25$ (R1-Distill) to $0.79$ (gpt-4o/4.1/Gemini); see Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}.}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.98 & 49 & 3.85 & $-$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 73.91 & 53 & 2.94 & $-$1.80 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 35 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). Symbolic confirmation bias reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\looseness=-1 Reasoning-process overlap exceeds answer-level correlation: on Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean process similarity (pairwise TF-IDF cosine; Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}) reaches 0.69, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with process similarity ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}).

\subsection{Why Remediation Fails}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:diagnostics}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free remediation strategies spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). The pattern reveals a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off: methods that successfully break trace correlation simultaneously destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy fail to achieve independence.

\looseness=-1 Four representative failure modes illustrate this trade-off. \emph{Debate} ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.6$\,pp) successfully breaks trace correlation but collapses accuracy: same-model diversity and quality are at odds. \emph{Cross-model extraction} (using a different LLM as extractor) decreases $\kappa$ slightly but $\Delta \approx 0$, because same-family models share systematic biases (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). \emph{Contrastive prompting} produces imbalanced output (94 pro vs.\ 65 con constraints), indicating confirmation bias transfers to constraint generation. \emph{Iterative constraint generation}, which refines constraints across multiple extraction rounds, reduces surface-level duplication but the refined constraints still align with the model's majority answer.

\looseness=-1 All non-debate strategies cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling observed across three families. This ceiling is not a property of any single extraction method but of the model itself: regardless of prompting strategy, self-extraction cannot produce constraints that are independent of the model's distributional prior. The oracle ceiling ($+29.90$\,pp with gold constraints) proves the mechanism is sound; self-extraction captures only ${\sim}$12\%, localizing the bottleneck to extraction quality rather than the MAX-SAT apparatus.

\looseness=-1 No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}). The consistent failure across 27 strategies, four categories, and three model families suggests that symbolic confirmation bias is not a fixable extraction artifact but an intrinsic property of self-referential constraint generation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_interventions_compact.pdf}
\caption{Twenty-three of 27 training-free strategies across four categories (four additional strategies tested but omitted for clarity). All produce neutral-to-negative $\Delta$ vs.\ SC. Grey band: $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Diagnostic Framework}
\label{sec:diagnostic-framework}

\looseness=-1 Three zero-cost diagnostics predict the outcome of any trace-aggregation method from a $K = 12$ pilot run: (1) the degenerate-regime indicator $C_0 < 5\%$ (SICA reduces to SC); (2) the diversity floor $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.3$ (insufficient vote diversity for any aggregation gain); (3) the bias ratio BR combined with SC regime (Type~A: SC $\geq 60\%$ with BR $> 1$ predicts confirmation-bias degradation; Type~C: SC $\leq 40\%$ predicts error amplification). These boundaries correctly classify all 60 tested conditions across four model families and three datasets.

\looseness=-1 Decision boundaries ($C_0 < 5\%$, $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.3$, SC $< 40\%$) were derived from Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-14B conditions. We evaluate prospectively on held-out LLaMA-3.1-8B, a different model family not used in boundary derivation: on FOLIO (SC $= 66.18\%$, $C_0 = 61.8\%$, $K_\text{eff} = 2.17$), the framework predicts Type~A confirmation bias ($\Delta \leq 0$); the observed $\Delta = -4.41$\,pp confirms the prediction.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. Under correlated traces (Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa}), effective sample size reduces to
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = K \,/\, [1 + (K{-}1) \cdot \kappa]
\end{equation}
(following \citet{ladha1992correlated}), motivating constraint-based aggregation.

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts logical constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}); in practice, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence (global average deduplication ratio: 46.0\%); each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout. Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0 = |\{q : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}| / |\mathcal{Q}|$ is a zero-cost diagnostic: $C_0 < 5\%$ means SICA reduces to SC. $\alpha = 0.5$ is not critical (${\pm}0.49$\,pp across $\alpha \in [0, 1]$).

\paragraph{Bias ratio.} We quantify extraction bias via the \emph{bias ratio}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mirage}
\text{BR}(M,T) = \frac{P(c \text{ supports } a_M \mid a_M \neq a^*)}{P(c \text{ supports } a^* \mid a_M \neq a^*)},
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a self-extracted constraint, $a_M$ the model's majority answer, and $a^*$ the ground truth. $\text{BR} > 1$ indicates constraints project model bias rather than provide independent verification; $\text{BR} = 1$ indicates unbiased extraction. We validate this metric empirically in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Models.} Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Qwen3-14B (non-thinking mode)~\citep{yang2024qwen25}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3~\citep{jiang2023mistral}, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{dubey2024llama3}, and QwQ-32B~\citep{qwen2024qwq} (7B--32B). We also evaluate the universality of symbolic confirmation bias via cross-model extraction on five frontier models (gpt-4o, gpt-4.1, gpt-5.5, o3, Gemini-2.5-Pro; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}); Mistral-7B extracts constraints from target traces since closed-source APIs do not support self-extraction.

\paragraph{Datasets.} FOLIO~\citep{han2022folio} ($n{=}204$, with gold logical annotations), ProofWriter depth-5~\citep{tafjord2021proofwriter} ($n{=}600$), LogiQA~\citep{liu2020logiqa} ($n{=}200$). StrategyQA~\citep{geva2021strategyqa} ($n{=}200$) serves as a domain-formalizability diagnostic. GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (exploratory, $n{=}30$) and AIME 2024 ($n{=}22$) probe the degenerate regime ($C_0 < 5\%$).

\paragraph{Parameters.} $K{=}12$ traces at $T{=}0.7$, extraction at $T_{\text{ext}}{=}0.3$, $\alpha{=}0.5$, Z3 timeout 10s. McNemar exact test ($\alpha{=}0.05$); 95\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples) for $n \geq 200$. SICA incurs $4.5{\times}$ SC wall-clock; extraction accounts for 76\% of overhead.

