\section{The Symbolic Mirage}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically align with the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. A shuffle control reveals this bias is \emph{structural}: randomizing constraint--answer associations while preserving per-trace counts yields $\text{BR}_\text{null} \approx \text{BR}_\text{obs}$ ($p > 0.7$), indicating that BR reflects the trace-count distribution rather than extraction-specific error. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, and MAX-SAT produces answers identical to count-only aggregation on 94.4\% of problems (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), confirming that the formal verification layer is vacuous (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}): 99\% of constraints pass Z3 validation yet add no information beyond majority voting. This vacuity is $\alpha$-insensitive: agreement $\geq$99\% across $\alpha \in \{0.1, \ldots, 0.9\}$ (Table~\ref{tab:alpha-ablation}).

\looseness=-1 This pattern generalizes: $4.97\times$ (Mistral), $3.85\times$ (Qwen-14B), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA), all $> 1.0$ with 95\% bootstrap CIs (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}). Cross-model extraction confirms universality across nine models from 7B to frontier (BR $1.66$--$4.97\times$; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}). BR $> 1$ further holds across all eight cells of a $2{\times}2$ generator${\times}$extractor matrix on FOLIO and ProofWriter (range $1.94$--$2.70$; Appendix~\ref{app:2x2}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Symbolic confirmation bias across nine models on FOLIO ($n = 204$). BR: mean per-problem bias ratio. $n_\text{err}$: number of incorrect instances on which BR is computed. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). Ext.: $^a$self-extraction; $^b$cross-model (Mistral-7B extracts FOL from target traces). $^*$Interim ($n = 95/204$). All models exhibit $\text{BR} > 1$; BR estimates for high-accuracy models ($n_\text{err} < 40$) have wider confidence intervals; Gemini ($n_\text{err} \approx 12$) should be interpreted with particular caution. Process-level similarity ($\kappa_{\text{proc}}$) ranges from $0.25$ (R1-Distill) to $0.79$ (gpt-4o/4.1/Gemini); see Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}.}
\label{tab:frontier-scb}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Ext. & SC (\%) & $n_\text{err}$ & BR & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral-7B & $^a$ & 55.88 & 90 & 4.97 & 0.00 \\
LLaMA-8B & $^a$ & \textrm{---} & \textrm{---} & 2.88 & \textrm{---} \\
Qwen-14B & $^a$ & 75.98 & 49 & 3.85 & $-$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
R1-Distill-8B & $^b$ & 74.51 & 52 & 2.92 & $+$2.45 \\
gpt-4o & $^a$ & 73.91 & 53 & 2.94 & $-$1.80 \\
gpt-4.1 & $^b$ & 82.84 & 35 & 2.70 & $-$1.47 \\
gpt-5.5 & $^b$ & 84.31 & 32 & 3.48 & $+$0.49 \\
o3 & $^b$ & 82.65 & 35 & 1.66 & 0.00 \\
Gemini-2.5-Pro$^*$ & $^b$ & 87.37 & 12 & 2.79 & $-$1.10 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. BR is uniform across early, mid, and late reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Removing the answer sentence from traces before extraction yields no BR decrease (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). Symbolic confirmation bias reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\looseness=-1 Reasoning-process overlap exceeds answer-level correlation: on Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean process similarity (pairwise TF-IDF cosine; Appendix~\ref{app:process-gradient}) reaches 0.69, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}). Under cross-model extraction, SICA gain decreases monotonically with process similarity ($\rho = -1.0$, $p = 0.008$; Table~\ref{tab:process-gradient}).

\subsection{Why Remediation Fails}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:diagnostics}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 All 27 training-free remediation strategies spanning four categories produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). The pattern reveals a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off: methods that successfully break trace correlation simultaneously destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy fail to achieve independence.

\looseness=-1 Four representative failure modes illustrate this trade-off. \emph{Debate} ($\kappa = 0.15$, $\Delta = -18.6$\,pp) successfully breaks trace correlation but collapses accuracy: same-model diversity and quality are at odds. \emph{Cross-model extraction} (using a different LLM as extractor) decreases $\kappa$ slightly but $\Delta \approx 0$, because same-family models share systematic biases (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). \emph{Contrastive prompting} produces imbalanced output (94 pro vs.\ 65 con constraints), indicating confirmation bias transfers to constraint generation. \emph{Iterative constraint generation}, which refines constraints across multiple extraction rounds, reduces surface-level duplication but the refined constraints still align with the model's majority answer.

\looseness=-1 All non-debate strategies cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, a same-model ceiling observed across three families. This ceiling is not a property of any single extraction method but of the model itself: regardless of prompting strategy, self-extraction cannot produce constraints that are independent of the model's distributional prior. The oracle ceiling ($+29.90$\,pp with gold constraints) proves the mechanism is sound; self-extraction captures only ${\sim}$12\%, localizing the bottleneck to extraction quality rather than the MAX-SAT apparatus.

\looseness=-1 No individual strategy achieves significance after correction (Appendix~\ref{app:intervention-individual}). The consistent failure across 27 strategies, four categories, and three model families suggests that symbolic confirmation bias is not a fixable extraction artifact but an intrinsic property of self-referential constraint generation.

